=80=99ve ever had. That itself is telling.</span></p><p style=3D"font:norma=
l 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The other notab=
le thing to me is the discrepancy between the personal affinity with Europe=
 most of you describe, and the frustration with the E.U. many feel.</span><=
/p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15=
px"><span>Ahead of next week=E2=80=99s State of the Union address by the pr=
esident of the European Commission, I want to share some of your comments (=
Ursula, I hope you=E2=80=99re reading).</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 1=
7px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>For some of you, t=
he feeling of being European actually trumps a sense of national identity. =
Britta Pulz-Fr=C3=B6hlich feels Schleswig-Holsteiner first (=E2=80=9CThis i=
s my home, my culture and my people=E2=80=9D) and European second. =E2=80=
=9CFunnily, I don=E2=80=99t really feel a =E2=80=98German=E2=80=99 identity=
,=E2=80=9D she writes; she has more in common with Danes just across the bo=
rder than Bavarians 700 kilometers south.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal=
 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Britta=E2=80=99s=
 Europe is one where regions rather than nations blend into one another, gi=
ving =E2=80=9Ca gradual change of cultures geographically from north to sou=
th and east to west, which in the end still just feel like different flavor=
s of =E2=80=98European,=E2=80=99 in Finland (Europe with cold weather) as m=
uch as in Portugal (Europe with warm weather).=E2=80=9D</span></p><p style=
=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>I=
 love this.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color=
:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>To some, Europe=E2=80=99s freedom of movement =
=E2=80=94 the ability to live and work in 27 different countries =E2=80=94 =
is convenient rather than identity-forging: Richard Funke, who lives in Bel=
gium with an Italian partner, said cross-border mobility had taken away the=
 =E2=80=9Cforeignness,=E2=80=9D but he doesn=E2=80=99t think =E2=80=9Cthis =
makes people suddenly proud Europeans.=E2=80=9D</span></p><p style=3D"font:=
normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>I also sen=
sed some complicated feelings from eastern European readers. Genika Grigoro=
va from Bulgaria fears that some economic and cultural divides between east=
 and west are =E2=80=9Cunbridgeable.=E2=80=9D They =E2=80=9Calways remind y=
ou that you are not really a part of Europe,=E2=80=9D she wrote.</span></p>=
